    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Biodiversity loss is an ongoing worldwide crisis: over 100 vertebrate species have gone extinct in the last 100 years alone, far higher than pre-human rates of loss (Johnson et al., 2017). Arguments to conserve biodiversity based on its concrete benefits to humans are often most compelling to decision-makers (Braat & de Groot, 2012). One remarkable example of a such a benefit is the economic revenue generated by ecotourism to observe biodiverse ecosystems. Costa Rica contains high levels of biodiversity (Myers et al., 2000) with a well-developed ecotourism industry (Instituto Costariccense de Turismo, 2017a). We aimed to deepen understanding of the connection between bird biodiversity and ecotourism visitation in Costa Rica by answering the following questions: 1) What is the spatial distribution of bird biodiversity across Costa Rica?; and 2) Is bird biodiversity a good predictor of ecotourism visitation rates in Costa Rica? We used MaxEnt (a range map modelling software) to model the distribution of over 650 Costa Rican bird species. We aggregated individual range maps into 6 different metrics of bird biodiversity based on total species richness, threatened species richness, and endemic species richness. We used protected area visitation as an on-the-ground metric of tourist visitation, and used photo uploads on the social media site Flickr and bird checklist uploads on the birding app eBird to assess tourism value spatially. We found that park visitation was significantly positively correlated with both total species richness and regional endemic species richness. Our analysis using MaxEnt revealed that total bird species diversity as a strong predictive factor for both Flickr photo upload locations and eBird bird checklist upload locations. Our results strongly support the hypothesis that bird biodiversity promotes tourism in Costa Rica.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
